
========================================================================
  PART 1: RSK(T) = RSK(T^op) -- THE COMPLEMENT INVARIANCE
========================================================================

  s164b found: RSK(T^op) = RSK(T) for ALL 64 tournaments at n=4.
  Does this hold at n=3 and n=5?

  n=3: RSK shape match 8/8 (100.0%), P-tableau match 0/8
  n=4: RSK shape match 64/64 (100.0%), P-tableau match 0/64
  n=5: RSK shape match 1024/1024 (100.0%), P-tableau match 0/1024

  ANALYSIS: RSK SHAPE is complement-invariant at n=3,4.
  Does it hold at n=5?

  n=5: RSK shape match 1024/1024 (100.0%)

  WHY RSK(T) and RSK(T^op) have the SAME SHAPE:
  For a 0-1 matrix M with M + M^T = J - I:
    RSK(M) has shape lambda
    RSK(M^T) has shape lambda' (conjugate)
    RSK(J-I-M) = RSK(M^T) since J-I-M = M^T
  Wait: J-I-M = M^T for tournaments!
  So RSK(T^op) = RSK(M^T) and this has shape lambda' = conjugate.

  But the OUTPUT says the shapes MATCH (not conjugate).
  This means: lambda = lambda' for all tournament RSK shapes!
  i.e., all RSK shapes of tournaments are SELF-CONJUGATE.

  n=5: Non-self-conjugate RSK shapes: 994 / 1024
  n=4: Non-self-conjugate RSK shapes: 50 / 64
  n=3: Non-self-conjugate RSK shapes: 2 / 8

========================================================================
  PART 2: ARC SENSITIVITY IS UNIVERSAL
========================================================================

  s164b found: mean|dH| = 3.000 for ALL arcs at n=5.
  This is remarkable: every arc, regardless of which pair (a,b),
  has the SAME average impact on H when flipped.

  PROOF ATTEMPT:
  By symmetry: S_n acts on tournaments by relabeling vertices.
  This action sends arc (a,b) to arc (sigma(a), sigma(b)).
  For ANY two arcs (a1,b1) and (a2,b2), there exists sigma in S_n
  with sigma(a1)=a2, sigma(b1)=b2 (S_n is 2-transitive on pairs).
  Since relabeling preserves H, the mean|dH| must be the same
  for all arcs. QED.

  This is a TRIVIAL consequence of vertex-label symmetry!
  The key insight: when averaging over ALL tournaments,
  every arc is equivalent by the S_n symmetry.

  Does universality hold in the TILING MODEL (base path fixed)?
  Non-base arc sensitivity in tiling model (n=5):
    arc(2,0) hook=5: mean|dH|=2.8750, max|dH|=8
    arc(3,0) hook=3: mean|dH|=2.7500, max|dH|=8
    arc(3,1) hook=3: mean|dH|=2.0000, max|dH|=6
    arc(4,0) hook=1: mean|dH|=4.2500, max|dH|=12
    arc(4,1) hook=1: mean|dH|=2.7500, max|dH|=8
    arc(4,2) hook=1: mean|dH|=2.8750, max|dH|=8

  In the tiling model, arc sensitivity is NOT universal!
  The base path breaks S_n symmetry, and different arcs have
  different mean |dH| values.


